    "topic_id": 42549,
    "poster_id": 1991,
    "post_rendered": "Let real coefficient polynomial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/3/333d3318fd0918987ca2c331f730e4ff8005d2e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = x^n + a_1 \\cdot x^{n-1} + \\ldots + a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"251\" height=\"19\" > has real roots <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/7/9e712ff3c1efcd54de88fdbe99450fed2d84ad55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_1, b_2, \\ldots, b_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"16\" >,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/1/2517cdd667e3022e0f35cbfdf13a0b8e40714479.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\geq 2,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" > prove that <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/7/8e76b11d9e1d81db3bd3757720658987f0235a04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\forall x \\geq max\\{b_1, b_2, \\ldots, b_n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"193\" height=\"18\" >,</span> we have<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/0/8008bc66e8b97afacf38c35f59968a4ac14b9f7d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(x+1) \\geq \\frac{2 \\cdot n^2}{\\frac{1}{x-b_1} + \\frac{1}{x-b_2} + \\ldots + \\frac{1}{x-b_n}}.\\]\"  width=\"290\" height=\"47\" >",
    "post_canonical": "Let real coefficient polynomial $f(x) = x^n + a_1 \\cdot x^{n-1} + \\ldots + a_n$ has real roots $b_1, b_2, \\ldots, b_n$, $n \\geq 2,$ prove that $\\forall x \\geq max\\{b_1, b_2, \\ldots, b_n\\}$, we have\r\n\\[f(x+1) \\geq \\frac{2 \\cdot n^2}{\\frac{1}{x-b_1} + \\frac{1}{x-b_2} + \\ldots + \\frac{1}{x-b_n}}.\\]",
    "username": "orl",
    "post_number": 1,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_1991.jpg"
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": 269212,
   "item_text": "Click for solution",
   "item_forum_id": 0,
   "item_type": "post_hidden",
   "in_feed": null,
   "is_watched": null,
   "item_score": "996",
   "id": "27469",
   "item_level": "996",
   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 269212,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 42549,
    "poster_id": 285,
    "post_rendered": "Put <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/2/5e246ce4838fc8e8f533f20fed3b03c5cd840738.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-b_i=a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"84\" height=\"15\" >.</span> We must prove that for positive numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/2/412787c048e28774dc63fc27db42dc52ca858de7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" > we have <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/8/b28ac96be923326d3fbe8985258a11617c04c001.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ (1+a_1)...(1+a_n)(1/a_1+...+1/a_n)\\geq 2n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"338\" height=\"19\" >.</span> USe Huygens inequality to find that the LHS is greater than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/4/73434f37e0932d01608232bd12712d8582fd44ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n(1+G)^n/G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"102\" height=\"18\" > where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > is the geometric mean of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/2/412787c048e28774dc63fc27db42dc52ca858de7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" >.</span> A study of the function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/7/2c76a340f099e0f7c0453e22a821e989b6b77d9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(1+x)^n}{x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"67\" height=\"38\" > shows that it has minimal value in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/7/c177dbcf45380be1d22a5759e5a1fcfbf4cb944a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" > and this one is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/f/48f1ca340f93e6298a44436c2bdc06ffe1a87dbc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ (n-1)(n/(n-1))^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"154\" height=\"18\" > obviously greater than <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/3/f93f5c51ea4b04b4992b003b39479f29018f6bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n$\"  width=\"19\" height=\"12\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "Put $x-b_i=a_i$. We must prove that for positive numbers $a_i$ we have $ (1+a_1)...(1+a_n)(1/a_1+...+1/a_n)\\geq 2n^2$. USe Huygens inequality to find that the LHS is greater than or equal to $n(1+G)^n/G$ where $G$ is the geometric mean of $a_i$. A study of the function $\\frac{(1+x)^n}{x}$ shows that it has minimal value in $\\frac{1}{n-1}$ and this one is $ (n-1)(n/(n-1))^n$ obviously greater than $2n$.",
    "username": "harazi",
    "post_number": 2,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_285.png"
   }
