   "item_score": "990",
   "id": "27475",
   "item_level": "990",
   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 269083,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 42554,
    "poster_id": 1991,
    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" > be a function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/2/232f76e4adbaf4f6562e4f4681ca2433a1f82a10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f: \\mathbb{N} \\cup \\{0\\} \\mapsto \\mathbb{N},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"131\" height=\"18\" > and satisfies the following conditions:<br>\n<br>\n(1) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/e/34ec4c732085e2b8b9a77477c6a3f7aa0720053f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(0) = 0, f(1) = 1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"147\" height=\"18\" ><br>\n(2) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/5/3c5c085784859fe37210487c2371e2e608688b86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(n+2) = 23 \\cdot f(n+1) + f(n), n = 0,1, \\ldots.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"353\" height=\"18\" ><br>\n<br>\nProve that for any <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/1/f21b49d4a1d07db0896300cd7c894fdbb50883a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\in \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"13\" >,</span> there exist a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/8/f485bf6620e1b600e7c0845e6c48563dc9d2044f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d \\in \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" > such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/8/5682598984a9c88fa1e1a061b4060a55ce2feb42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m | f(f(n)) \\Leftrightarrow d | n.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"139\" height=\"18\" >",
    "post_canonical": "Let $f$ be a function $f: \\mathbb{N} \\cup \\{0\\} \\mapsto \\mathbb{N},$ and satisfies the following conditions:\r\n\r\n(1) $f(0) = 0, f(1) = 1,$\r\n(2) $f(n+2) = 23 \\cdot f(n+1) + f(n), n = 0,1, \\ldots.$\r\n\r\nProve that for any $m \\in \\mathbb{N}$, there exist a $d \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that $m | f(f(n)) \\Leftrightarrow d | n.$",
    "username": "orl",
    "post_number": 1,
